  {
   "id": "QAdOUkRmtvsJ",
   "from": "Kalyan K Pasumarthy",
   "author": "e4181d5c40fc094c",
   "date": "2014-04-05T04:29:26Z",
   "subject": "Re: [methylkit_discussion] Histogram of % methylation plot",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>Hi,<br><br></div>Your data contains Cs from every possible context. Your plot suggests that 99.8% of Cs are unmethylated (Actually the picture is very small and fonts are difficult to read). So, there are 0.2% Cs that are methylated. To me it seems that the proportion of methylated Cs is too small to be seen on the plot!<br>\r\n\r\n<br></div>If you are working on human genome (not mentioned in your email), consider the fact that genome has very few CpG and among them a higher proportion of them are unmethylated. And a huge majority of Cs in other contexts are also unmethylated (too huge to be compared with methylated Cs in other contexts in terms of numbers). Since your data is dominated by unmethylated Cytosines, it is not surprising to see such a plot! Generally, researchers speak about cytosine methylation in terms of a particular context (not necessary to be followed).<br>\r\n\r\n<br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\">Regards,<br>Kalyan<br></div></div>\r\n<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\"></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On 4 April 2014 23:49,  <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">him...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br></div></div>"
    ],
    [
