   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">I think the main problem could be that you are not reading the file correctly, there are blogposts at <a href=\"http://zvfak.blogspot.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://zvfak.blogspot.com&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1793234576208000&amp;usg=AOvVaw2qFUrZsBF5fHRcePsdfWPL\">zvfak.blogspot.com</a> and code in this forum showing how to read bsmap files, or any other generic methylation ratio/percentage per base file. <div>\r\n<br></div><div>In addition, Kalyan is right. Assuming, you are reading the file correctly, you would see a lot of unmethylated Cs, but I would also expect a small bump towards the end.</div><div><br></div><div>Best,</div>\r\n<div>Altuna</div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Sat, Apr 5, 2014 at 6:29 AM, Kalyan K Pasumarthy <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">kaly...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br>\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\"><div dir=\"ltr\"><div><div><div>Hi,<br><br></div>Your data contains Cs from every possible context. Your plot suggests that 99.8% of Cs are unmethylated (Actually the picture is very small and fonts are difficult to read). So, there are 0.2% Cs that are methylated. To me it seems that the proportion of methylated Cs is too small to be seen on the plot!<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<br></div>If you are working on human genome (not mentioned in your email), consider the fact that genome has very few CpG and among them a higher proportion of them are unmethylated. And a huge majority of Cs in other contexts are also unmethylated (too huge to be compared with methylated Cs in other contexts in terms of numbers). Since your data is dominated by unmethylated Cytosines, it is not surprising to see such a plot! Generally, researchers speak about cytosine methylation in terms of a particular context (not necessary to be followed).<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n<br></div></div><div class=\"gmail_extra\"><br clear=\"all\"><div><div dir=\"ltr\">Regards,<br>Kalyan<br></div></div><div><div>\r\n<br><br><div class=\"gmail_quote\">On 4 April 2014 23:49,  <span dir=\"ltr\">&lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">him...@gmail.com</a>&gt;</span> wrote:<br><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n\r\n\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Thank you for developing a nice tool!</div><div><br></div>I have a Whole genome bisulfite sequence file that was mapped with bsmap-2.73.  I did not separate the CpG from CHG or CHH.<div>The input file for MethylKit looks like this.</div>\r\n\r\n\r\n<div><br></div><div><div>chr<span style